const executed = await executePreparedToolCall(preparation, signal, emit);
```
Extension tools vs built-ins: SAME code path, no separate scheduler. Extension tools are converted to plain AgentTools by `pkg/dist/core/extensions/wrapper.js` (`wrapRegisteredTool` → `wrapToolDefinition`, `pkg/dist/core/tools/tool-definition-wrapper.js:9-12` copies `executionMode`), and the dispatch decision reads the tool from `currentContext.tools` regardless of origin. Built-ins (`read|bash|powershell|edit|write|grep|find|ls`, `pkg/dist/core/tools/index.js:18-27`) declare no `executionMode` and the coding-agent passes no `toolExecution` option, so nothing in the shipped product forces sequential — grep for `executionMode:`/`"sequential"` across both packages returns only the type declaration (`pkg/dist/core/extensions/types.d.ts:364-370`, `.../pi-agent-core/dist/types.d.ts:359`) and the harness default (`.../pi-agent-core/dist/harness/runtime/harness.js:46` `toolExecution: options.toolExecution ?? "parallel"`). Practical consequence: an extension merely adds `executionMode: "sequential"` to ONE tool of a batch to serialize the whole batch (documented at `pkg/dist/core/extensions/types.d.ts:365`: "this tool must execute one at a time with other tool calls").

One ordering nuance: in the parallel path `beforeToolCall` (extension `tool_call` hook) and argument validation run sequentially in message order before any body starts (`agent-loop.js:332-345` → `prepareToolCall`, `agent-loop.js:~400-450`), and "immediate" outcomes (unknown tool, blocked, validation error) are appended in order without being scheduled (`agent-loop.js:340-351`).

