One ordering nuance: in the parallel path `beforeToolCall` (extension `tool_call` hook) and argument validation run sequentially in message order before any body starts (`agent-loop.js:332-345` → `prepareToolCall`, `agent-loop.js:~400-450`), and "immediate" outcomes (unknown tool, blocked, validation error) are appended in order without being scheduled (`agent-loop.js:340-351`).

2) Per-message concurrency cap / global tool queue
NO cap and NO global queue in this path. The parallel branch is an unbounded `Promise.all` over every toolCall in the message (`agent-loop.js:372`); grep for `mutex|semaphore|concurrency|p-limit|withLock` across `pkg/dist/core/extensions/runner.js`, `pkg/dist/core/agent-session.js` returns nothing. The only serialization primitives found are:
- `pkg/dist/core/tools/file-mutation-queue.js:28-32` — a per-file (realpath-keyed) mutation queue used by the built-in `edit`/`write` tools (`withFileMutationQueue`); it is keyed on the file path, not on the tool batch, and does not affect extension tools.
- run-level single-flight: `Agent.runWithLifecycle` throws if another run is active (`.../pi-agent-core/dist/agent.js:326-328` `if (this.activeRun) throw new Error("Agent is already processing.")`).
Scholar's own per-(vault,bookId) queue therefore remains the only mutual exclusion for its book mutations — the host adds none.

3) Streaming vs awaiting all sibling results
All sibling results are awaited before the next model request. `agent-loop.js:138-145`:
```js
: await executeToolCalls(currentContext, message, config, signal, emit);
toolResults.push(...executedToolBatch.messages);
for (const result of toolResults) {
    currentContext.messages.push(result);
```
and only after that does `runLoop` iterate back into `streamAssistantResponse` (`agent-loop.js:~100-130`). Additionally, in the parallel branch the per-call tool-result messages are emitted only after `Promise.all` resolves — `agent-loop.js:372-377`:
```js
const orderedFinalizedCalls = await Promise.all(...);
const messages = [];
for (const finalized of orderedFinalizedCalls) {
    const toolResultMessage = createToolResultMessage(finalized);
    await emitToolResultMessage(toolResultMessage, emit);
```
What DOES stream incrementally is only UI/progress: tools may call `onUpdate`, which emits `tool_execution_update` events (`agent-loop.js:459-470`: `updateEvents.push(Promise.resolve(emit({ type: "tool_execution_update", ... partialResult })))`) and `tool_execution_start`/`tool_execution_end` per call. Consequence for the model context and session persistence: tool-result messages are appended (and `message_start`/`message_end`-persisted by AgentSession) only after the whole batch settles, in assistant-message call order, regardless of which sibling finished first.

4) Multiple image parts per tool result, and limits
Allowed by type: `.../pi-ai/dist/types.d.ts:330-334`
```ts
export interface ToolResultMessage<TDetails = any> {
  role: "toolResult";
  content: (TextContent | ImageContent)[];
```
The loop copies content verbatim, adding no cap — `agent-loop.js:~437-448` `createToolResultMessage`: `content: finalized.result.content ?? []` (no slicing, no byte check).
The only host-side validation/truncation of tool-result images is the global `afterToolCall` hook installed by AgentSession — `pkg/dist/core/agent-session.js:258-262`:
```js
const content = hookResult?.content ?? result.content ?? [];
// Runs after the extension hook so images injected or replaced by extensions are normalized too.
const normalizedContent = await normalizeToolResultImages(content, {
    autoResizeImages: this.settingsManager.getImageAutoResize(),
```
`pkg/dist/utils/tool-result-images.js` iterates the array and processes EACH image block independently; there is no count limit and no aggregate-byte limit:
```js
processed.push({ type: "image", data: processed.data, mimeType: processed.mimeType });
```
Per-image constants (`pkg/dist/utils/image-resize-core.js:5-10`):
```js
// 4.5MB of base64 payload. Provides headroom below Anthropic's 5MB limit.
const DEFAULT_MAX_BYTES = 4.5 * 1024 * 1024;
const DEFAULT_OPTIONS = { maxWidth: 2000, maxHeight: 2000, maxBytes: DEFAULT_MAX_BYTES, jpegQuality: 80 };
```
Gate: `autoResizeImages` defaults to true (`pkg/dist/core/settings-manager.js:891-892` `return this.settings.images?.autoResize ?? true;`). Format gate: only `image/png|jpeg|gif|webp` pass through; anything else is converted to PNG or rejected (`pkg/dist/utils/image-process.js:~29-45`). IMPORTANT SOFT SPOT: if resize/conversion fails, the ORIGINAL block is kept untouched (tool-result-images.js: `if (!processed.ok) { normalized.push(block); continue; }`) — i.e. an oversized/unresizable image is NOT dropped or truncated, it goes into history and every later provider request as-is; the commented rationale is that a failure may just mean the image backend is unavailable.
At provider conversion time every image part is forwarded, e.g. `.../pi-ai/dist/api/openai-completions.js:1093-1102` (loop over `toolMsg.content`, push `{type:"image_url", image_url:{url: `data:${block.mimeType};base64,${block.data}`}}`), and `.../pi-ai/dist/api/anthropic-messages.js:80-112` `convertContentBlocks` maps all image parts to base64 `image` blocks, inserting a `(see attached image)` text block when a result is image-only. Context accounting counts each image as `ESTIMATED_IMAGE_CHARS = 4800` chars (`pkg/dist/core/compaction/compaction.js:~172-180`).
For contrast, the 50KB/2000-line truncation constants (`DEFAULT_MAX_BYTES = 50 * 1024`, `DEFAULT_MAX_LINES = 2000`, `pkg/dist/core/tools/truncate.js:10-11`, mirrored in `.../pi-agent-core/dist/harness/utils/truncate.js`) are applied BY individual tools to their own text output, never by the loop to the tool-result message.
UNCONFIRMED / not present in host code: any hard provider-level ceiling (e.g. Anthropic's ~5MB per base64 image, max images per request, OpenAI payload limits) is not enforced here — the 4.5MB figure is only a resize heuristic, and an unprocessable image bypasses it. Also unconfirmed: whether some UI/renderer drops images (interactive renderer iterates ALL image blocks: `pkg/dist/modes/interactive/components/tool-execution.js:141-143` and `:289-293` — no cap seen).

5) Shared AbortSignal / turn context; sibling cancellation on failure
One AbortController per RUN (i.e. per prompt/turn lifecycle), shared by every sibling. `.../pi-agent-core/dist/agent.js:330-340`:
```js
const abortController = new AbortController();
...
await executor(abortController.signal);
```
That `signal` is threaded unchanged into the loop and into each call — `agent-loop.js:139` `executeToolCalls(currentContext, message, config, signal, emit)` → `agent-loop.js:363` `const executed = await executePreparedToolCall(preparation, signal, emit);` → tool signature `execute(toolCallId, params, signal, onUpdate)` (types.d.ts:372). The same signal is also handed to extension hook listeners (`agent.js:413-419`: `const signal = this.activeRun?.abortController.signal; ... await listener(event, signal)`) and to extension context (`pkg/dist/core/agent-session.js:2063` `getSignal: () => this.agent.signal`).
No sibling cancellation on failure: a throwing tool is converted into an error result, not a cancellation — `agent-loop.js:479-485`
```js
catch (error) {
    acceptingUpdates = false;
    await Promise.all(updateEvents);
    return { result: createErrorToolResult(error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)), isError: true };
```
Same for post-processing hook failures (`finalizeExecutedToolCall` catch) and for `beforeToolCall` throws (`prepareToolCall` catch → `kind: "immediate"`). The only abort-driven interruption is the run-level signal: `agent.js:202-204` `abort() { this.activeRun?.abortController.abort(); }` (triggered by user interrupt / `AgentSession.abort()` at `pkg/dist/core/agent-session.js:1225-1226`, dispose at :589-590, compaction at :1474-1475), and the loop only checks it between/around calls — `agent-loop.js:368-370`
```js
if (signal?.aborted) {
    break;
```
plus pre-flight checks inside each task (`agent-loop.js:354-362` returns `Operation aborted` for a call that has not started yet). Already-running siblings are NOT cancelled when a later sibling in the same batch fails; conversely, `abort()` cancels all in-flight siblings simultaneously because they share the signal. `terminate: true` on a result only stops the outer loop from issuing another assistant turn (`shouldTerminateToolBatch`, `agent-loop.js:~380-383`) — it does not cancel siblings in the same batch.

Bottom line for the Scholar extension: instructing the model to emit several independent `scholar` calls in one assistant message is supported and they WILL run concurrently on the host (extension tools use the same `Promise.all` path; only a per-tool `executionMode: "sequential"` opt-in would serialize them), the host imposes no cap or queue, all results are collected in call order before the next model request, and multiple `{type:"image"}` parts in one tool result are legal and forwarded. Caveats to design around: (a) images are resized per block to <=4.5MB base64 / 2000px / JPEG q80, but a block that cannot be processed passes through unchecked; (b) the whole batch is an all-or-nothing barrier for the model context, so a slow sibling delays the next model turn; (c) no host-side per-(vault,bookId) mutex exists, so concurrent sibling calls that mutate the same book must be serialized by the scholar tool itself."}, "error": null, "type": null}

